direction: down

title: |md
  ## When offloads bite you
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

bpf: "BPF (XDP, tc-bpf)" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  b_body: |md
    GRO ON: program sees one big skb,\
    not the original wire segments.

    Effects:
    - Latency tracing per-packet → wrong
    - Packet count metrics → low
    - Per-segment flags lost

    Many BPF programs disable GRO on\
    the NIC they trace.
  |
}

tcp: "TCP timing measurements" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  t_body: |md
    GRO holds segments briefly to\
    coalesce. Adds 10-100 µs latency\
    on bursts. Mostly invisible at\
    application layer.

    For latency-critical workloads\
    (HFT, RDMA), GRO is OFF.
  |
}

cap: "Packet capture / IDS" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  c_body: |md
    tcpdump on RX sees pre-stack —\
    so it observes the GRO\
    superpackets too! Use\
    `ethtool -K eth0 gro off`\
    if you need wire-fidelity captures.

    Or capture before driver-receive\
    via XDP-pass-through.
  |
}

verdict: "ethtool -K is the toggle" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  v_body: |md
    Default settings on modern Linux:
    - GRO on
    - GSO on
    - TSO on (if HW supports)

    Disable as needed:\
    `sudo ethtool -K eth0 gro off`
  |
}

bpf -> tcp -> cap -> verdict
